content:
An upload picker is a usability feature, not a trust boundary. A product-image tool should reject unsuitable inputs early in the browser and repeat the important checks on the server. This guide builds the browser half and specifies the server contract without pretending that a file extension proves a format.

## Define a small, explicit input contract

For this example, accept one PNG or JPEG up to 8 MiB and up to 20 million decoded pixels. These are example application limits, not format limits or universal security guarantees. Choose production limits from measured worker memory, supported devices and your threat model. A compressed file can be small while its decoded bitmap is large.

Use a named file input, an associated label and a status element. The `accept` attribute helps users choose a file. A user can still supply a different file, so server validation remains necessary.

```html
<label for="photo">Product photo: PNG or JPEG</label>
<input id="photo" type="file" accept="image/png,image/jpeg">
<p id="result" role="status"></p>
<script type="module" src="/upload-check.js"></script>
```

The script below checks a few identification bytes before decoding. A matching signature is only a preliminary check; it does not prove that all chunks are valid or that the decoder is safe.

## Check and decode with bounded expectations

```javascript
const MAX_BYTES = 8 * 1024 * 1024;
const MAX_PIXELS = 20_000_000;
const input = document.querySelector('#photo');
const status = document.querySelector('#result');
let revision = 0;

async function checkImage(file) {
  if (!file || file.size === 0 || file.size > MAX_BYTES) {
    throw new Error('Choose a nonempty image no larger than 8 MiB.');
  }
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(await file.slice(0, 8).arrayBuffer());
  const png = [137, 80, 78, 71, 13, 10, 26, 10]
    .every((value, index) => bytes[index] === value);
  const jpeg = bytes[0] === 255 && bytes[1] === 216 && bytes[2] === 255;
  if (!png && !jpeg) throw new Error('The file does not look like PNG or JPEG.');
  const bitmap = await createImageBitmap(file);
  try {
    const { width, height } = bitmap;
    if (width < 1 || height < 1 || width * height > MAX_PIXELS) {
      throw new Error('Image dimensions exceed this tool’s limit.');
    }
    return { width, height, format: png ? 'PNG' : 'JPEG' };
  } finally {
    bitmap.close();
  }
}

input.addEventListener('change', async () => {
  const current = ++revision;
  status.textContent = 'Checking image…';
  try {
    const result = await checkImage(input.files?.[0]);
    if (current === revision) {
      status.textContent = `${result.format}: ${result.width} × ${result.height}`;
    }
  } catch {
    if (current === revision) status.textContent = 'Choose a supported, smaller image.';
  }
});
```

The revision counter prevents a slower previous selection from replacing a newer result. Closing the bitmap releases its graphics resources. The decoded-pixel check happens after allocation, so this browser example cannot guarantee a hard memory ceiling. Keep the server decoder isolated and bound its resources independently.

## Make the server authoritative

Enforce the request body limit while reading the stream; do not buffer an unlimited body and then check its length. Parse uploads with a maintained multipart implementation. Generate storage names yourself. Never use a client filename as a path. Verify the actual format with the decoder, reject unsupported features and dimensions, and re-encode accepted content into the specific output format your application supports.

Keep quarantine objects private and separate from public assets. Scan when required by the use case, but do not treat a scanner result as a substitute for decoder isolation. See [Building a Secure Image Processing Pipeline](../building-a-secure-image-processing-pipeline/) for the processing boundary.

## Verify the unhappy paths

Try a text file renamed to `.png`, a zero-byte file, a damaged PNG, an oversized upload, a very large compressed bitmap and two rapid consecutive selections. Confirm that rejected files never reach the public asset store. Test keyboard access and screen-reader status messages as well as successful uploads.

When preparing a cutout with [RemovePhotoBG](https://removephotobg.com/), apply the same independent validation to the exported file before importing it into your own workflow. This guide does not assume a public product API. Continue with [Transparent PNG and Alpha Channel Best Practices](../transparent-png-and-alpha-channel-best-practices/) to inspect the resulting transparency.

## Primary references

- [MDN: accept attribute](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Attributes/accept)
- [OWASP: File Upload Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/File_Upload_Cheat_Sheet.html)
